{{> Top}}<article class="prose">
<h1>Workloads</h1>
<p>Each workload is one part of a server&rsquo;s job. Every server must answer the same bytes, checked against a reference before it races. How a race runs and what it reports follow the workloads.</p>
{{?ready}}<div class="workloads">
{{#cards}}<section class="card"><h3>{{ title }} <code>{{ route }}</code></h3>
<p class="summary">{{ summary }}</p>
{{#paragraphs}}<p>{{ . }}</p>
{{/paragraphs}}<p class="variant protocols">{{#protocols}}<span>{{ . }}</span>{{/protocols}}</p></section>
{{/cards}}</div>
<h2>Variants</h2>
<p>Each test is raced in each of its variants: the same requests and the same answers, carried another way. The race page&rsquo;s tabs are HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 + TLS; h2c sits below either.</p>
{{#variants}}<h3 class="variant-name">{{ label }}</h3>
<p>{{ intro }}</p>
<ul class="variant-rows">{{#rows}}<li><span class="variant">{{#line}}<span>{{ . }}</span>{{/line}}</span> {{ tests }}</li>
{{/rows}}</ul>
{{/variants}}<p>Definitions: <a href="https://github.com/Eldhus/fourneau-dragrace/blob/master/race.json"><code>race.json</code></a>.</p>
{{/ready}}{{^ready}}<p class="pending">No race yet: the workloads show once the first has run.</p>
{{/ready}}
<h2>The race</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Machines.</b> Each server class gets a fresh server droplet and a loader droplet with more vCPUs, running <a href="https://github.com/hatoo/oha">oha</a>, in one region, over the private network. The classes race at the same time; every droplet is deleted afterwards.</li>
<li><b>Checks.</b> Before racing, <code>/plaintext</code> must answer exactly <code>Hello, World!</code>, <code>/echo</code> the body it was sent, <code>/menu</code> the reference page, <code>/sse</code> the reference stream with one chunk per event (and 400 without signals), and an unknown path 404; over TLS, TLS&nbsp;1.3 with HTTP/2 by ALPN; without TLS, <code>/plaintext</code> over h2c too. RealWorld&rsquo;s answers are checked against the seed&rsquo;s model before its climb. Each mode, with TLS and without, is checked on its own: a server that fails one is a DNF there and still races the other.</li>
<li><b>Builds.</b> Each server is built and configured the way its users ship it. Every dependency is pinned but fourneau and roux, which race their master; each race records their commits and the toolchains&rsquo; versions.</li>
<li><b>Noise.</b> Every round records CPU steal, and each machine its CPU model, so a night&rsquo;s hardware shows in its data.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Closed loop</h2>
<p>oha holds <i>N</i> connections (each workload&rsquo;s above), each sending its next request when the last answer lands, so every server runs flat out at its own pace. Each closed-loop workload (all but RealWorld) gets, in each of {{?ready}}{{ rounds }} {{/ready}}rounds of a seeded random order, a warmup{{?ready}} of {{ warmup }}&nbsp;s{{/ready}}, discarded, then a measured run{{?ready}} of {{ measure }}&nbsp;s{{/ready}}.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Throughput</b> is the best round&rsquo;s, as a benchmark takes the fastest of its repeats: a server can do no more than it did, and a round that fell short met noise (a neighbour, the network, a frequency change).</li>
<li><b>Each latency percentile</b> is the rounds&rsquo; median: a tail is the server&rsquo;s own rare stalls, so the least would report the round that missed them and the most the round a neighbour hit.</li>
</ul>
<p>By Little&rsquo;s law latency &asymp; <i>N</i> &divide; throughput: these are a saturated server&rsquo;s latencies, and they hide three things.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Coordinated omission.</b> A stall stalls the generator too; the requests users would have sent meanwhile never exist, so the tail is understated, most for servers that pause.</li>
<li><b>Latency at a given rate.</b> Each server runs at its own maximum, so no two latencies are at the same load.</li>
<li><b>The knee.</b> The loop sits at capacity throughout and never shows the climb to it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Open loop</h2>
<p>RealWorld races open loop only: oha offers fixed rates, the same for every server (its card gives them), each step about &radic;2 the last, and each server climbs until it falls behind. Latency is counted from when each request was due (<a href="https://github.com/giltene/wrk2">wrk2</a>&rsquo;s correction; oha <code>-q --latency-correction</code>). Steps, not a ramp, so each latency belongs to one rate.</p>
<p>A step with the loader {{ loader_limit }}% busy or more is drawn hollow.</p>

<h2>Caveats</h2>
<ul>
<li>The loader&rsquo;s CPU is recorded every round, so a round it bound shows.</li>
<li>Echo on the dedicated class can fill the network link first: the fast servers tie there (each round records the traffic).</li>
<li>Only 2xx answers count: an error or another status is no work done.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is a nightly on hardware anyone can rent (DigitalOcean picks the CPU; each class names it), not a best case on tuned bare metal. The same race runs on your own machine: <code>dragrace race local</code>.</p>
</article>
{{> Bottom}}
